WEBVTT

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:00.840
*Mumbling*

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:56.560
The Heimers from Otley and Yorkshire are giving up their normal lives to go back in time to the Second World War.

00:01:00.990 --> 00:01:09.200
Michael, an engineer, his wife, Lynn, their 29-year-old daughter Kirstie, and grandsons

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:16.270
Benjamin, aged 10, and Thomas, 7, will spend the next nine weeks living as a family on

00:01:16.270 --> 00:01:17.270
the home front.

00:01:17.270 --> 00:01:25.130
I don't wear dresses, I'm always a trouser person, so different, very different.

00:01:25.130 --> 00:01:27.810
Come in then, wherever you are.

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:48.510
It's an emotional moment. Our family are leaving their hometown for the first

00:01:48.510 --> 00:01:56.090
time. As I came out of the door I realised that this was it now, that I

00:01:56.090 --> 00:02:00.000
couldn't turn back. There were too many people involved and all the

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:06.250
of it was being set in motion and here we go for better or worse as it were thank you everybody

00:02:06.250 --> 00:02:12.330
thank you this is what i imagine was the hardest thing for the people who lived through the war

00:02:12.330 --> 00:02:18.980
people saying goodbye it must have been so so hard if it's going to be like this it's going

00:02:18.980 --> 00:02:20.480
to be an emotional roller coaster

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:20.800
and

00:02:30.990 --> 00:02:36.030
Ben was acting grown-up and trying not to let it, you know, bother him, but Thomas did actually get quite upset.

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:49.790
Can't even begin to imagine what it must have felt like to, you know, wave your husband off or even wave your children off, but people had to do it.

00:02:58.430 --> 00:03:00.000
If this were the summer of 1903,

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:05.500
1939, the Heimers would be joining over two and a quarter million Britons who were forced

00:03:05.500 --> 00:03:21.890
to leave home in the first month of war. In West Wickham, on the borders of London and

00:03:21.890 --> 00:03:30.000
Kent, the Heimers' new home is waiting. It's everything a middle-class home like this would

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:45.680
have been. No phone, no car, no central heating. In the 1940s house, life for the Heimers will

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:50.040
be strictly controlled. There's a war on, and they're going to feel the impact of a

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:55.180
strong government. Let's start with the big question. In Churchill's war rooms, we've

00:03:55.180 --> 00:04:00.000
assembled historians and scientists. From now on, they will dictate all

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:04.800
aspects of the family's experience. As the cabinet we're going to have to say

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:09.360
there were regulations, there were rules from authority that governed your life

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:12.720
in wartime and these are going to have to govern your life too. You're not going

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:16.640
to be able to have consultation, rationing is going to affect you, various

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:20.340
emergency powers are going to affect you and this is how you're going to have to

00:04:20.340 --> 00:04:23.100
live. Let's get their neighbors denouncing them to the air raid

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:27.230
precautions people, let's get them fined, let's get them, let's see the

00:04:27.230 --> 00:04:30.000
psychological impact of the war on

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:42.060
the home front. Waiting to meet the family is our consultant Marguerite Patton. She was a wartime

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:55.350
advisor for the Ministry of Food. I'm here just to show you into your new home. Can you get this?

00:04:55.350 --> 00:05:00.000
I say this. Splendid creature. Right, don't come in.

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:00.880
There we are.

00:05:02.380 --> 00:05:04.080
Oh, that's wonderful.

00:05:04.460 --> 00:05:06.480
By the name I mean, Smoggery Pet.

