00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:37.700
Excited about building a shelter. It's been Michael's dream since boyhood, but he'll have to pay seven pounds for the pleasure

00:20:37.740 --> 00:20:42.860
That's more than his week's earnings Anderson's were only free to those on low wages

00:20:44.070 --> 00:20:47.510
My dad is so happy

00:20:47.510 --> 00:20:52.740
Whether we'll be happy in two or three days when he's trying to build a water and you're sleeping on a sudden

00:20:52.740 --> 00:21:00.000
I don't care. It's what you've always wanted. I'm not the very long ago on reaching home. I found a load of corrugated iron somebody

00:21:00.060 --> 00:21:05.160
left around. According to instructions of the Save My Worthy Skin, the idea was to dig

00:21:05.160 --> 00:21:07.040
a hole and pick the sections in.

00:21:07.040 --> 00:21:10.840
Something I've always wanted to do, build an Anderson shelter.

00:21:10.840 --> 00:21:16.110
Keep your fingers out, just to see what was involved and how much work was entailed.

00:21:16.110 --> 00:21:20.870
That's what I'm trying to do here. Dig it in properly, kit it out, and sleep in it.

00:21:20.870 --> 00:21:23.020
Must be mad.

00:21:23.020 --> 00:21:30.000
So with my pick and shovel I began to excavate for my shelter at the bottom of the garden.

00:21:30.000 --> 00:21:34.500
are not the common sense advice, but my replies to some of them were not exactly nice.

00:21:34.500 --> 00:21:37.500
The ground was just like concrete and the shovel was antique.

00:21:37.500 --> 00:21:41.000
The pig went through a gas pipe and the gas began to leak.

00:21:41.000 --> 00:21:44.000
And when I called the plumber, his description was unique

00:21:44.000 --> 00:21:47.000
for my gentleman at the bottom of the garden.

00:21:47.000 --> 00:21:52.070
What it is, this, it's supposed to be a big pan of soup,

00:21:52.070 --> 00:21:54.150
which will last as a couple of days.

00:21:54.150 --> 00:21:56.150
What it actually is, is a big pan of grease.

00:21:56.150 --> 00:21:58.610
When I did the onions,

00:22:00.570 --> 00:22:19.230
I did them in lard, so when I put all the stock and everything in and the onions, the lard just made it this greasy mess, so I thought if I put it outside for a while it would go cold, and I'd be able to skim the lard off the top, but for some reason it's not solidifying.

00:22:20.080 --> 00:22:21.670
I'm not used to manual labour.

00:22:26.600 --> 00:22:30.000
Let's wash my spalls, or myself.

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:38.240
my father's largest. I mean how small is that? How small is that? Not very.

00:22:38.240 --> 00:22:49.890
Oh Dad, it's full of stone. So it's supposed to be big enough to get Thomas in.

00:22:49.890 --> 00:22:54.650
With the excitement of building the shelter, the more pressing job of doing the blackout

00:22:54.650 --> 00:23:00.000
gets put on hold. I think somebody needs to write a letter to the ARP to ask if

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:06.230
we can postpone tomorrow night's blackout check because I won't get this done in time well let's

00:23:06.230 --> 00:23:17.190
give it another hour and if you haven't finished the Anderson by then you mad bugger one penny to

00:23:17.190 --> 00:23:30.000
the swear box catch it before it falls in the hole if we've got to dig five times more further down this I'll be on my knees

00:23:30.280 --> 00:23:31.240
in a couple of hours

00:23:31.240 --> 00:23:32.920
because I just can't do this.

00:23:33.420 --> 00:23:34.660
And Dad can't do it on his own.

00:23:35.560 --> 00:23:36.880
He might like to think he can.

00:23:38.800 --> 00:23:40.440
Thomas, it's not helping with you

00:23:40.440 --> 00:23:42.260
standing on the bit that I'm trying to do.

00:23:43.340 --> 00:23:45.780
This is your fault, Dad.

00:23:45.800 --> 00:23:47.140
I'm not even a foot down yet.

00:23:48.140 --> 00:23:49.600
You've got four of them to go.

00:23:49.660 --> 00:23:50.180
Well, three.

00:23:50.480 --> 00:23:52.660
Three of what the depth it is now?

00:23:52.660 --> 00:23:53.420
Yeah, three feet.

00:23:53.420 --> 00:23:54.700
You've got to be joking, Dad.

00:23:54.740 --> 00:23:55.780
I can't do this.

00:23:55.950 --> 00:23:56.570
I'll do it.

00:23:56.650 --> 00:23:57.790
But you'll have a hard time.

00:23:57.810 --> 00:23:58.190
I'll do it.

00:23:58.310 --> 00:23:58.950
Yes, you will.

00:23:59.150 --> 00:24:00.000
It may take me a while.

00:24:00.060 --> 00:24:07.690
we're not gonna be able to do this yeah no no we are we've got to keep up the spirit that made

00:24:07.690 --> 00:24:15.120
England great you couldn't just in the water and sit back posing on a heap of soil like a girly

00:24:15.120 --> 00:24:30.000
postcard and say we're not going to be able to do this we will do it skimmed all the fat off it

00:24:30.030 --> 00:24:38.520
it's just a mass of salt because it was a bacon joint but they're so salty so as it's reduced

00:24:38.520 --> 00:24:48.220
it's got saltier and saltier and I have absolutely no idea what to do with it well it's hot Tom

00:24:48.220 --> 00:24:57.970
it's salty good god mum no don't eat it children it'll make them poorly what I would say is it's

00:24:57.970 --> 00:24:59.770
um awful

00:25:00.120 --> 00:25:04.940
It's very, very salty. It's like drinking, having a mouthwash or whatever you do.

00:25:04.940 --> 00:25:09.040
Okay, so it's back to the old standby. Bread and jam.

00:25:09.040 --> 00:25:11.170
Oh honey. Sorry about that.

00:25:11.170 --> 00:25:13.080
Please let your pass me down.

00:25:13.080 --> 00:25:15.540
The blackout stuff is not going to get done.

00:25:15.540 --> 00:25:21.060
The shelter is proving a bigger job than expected, and there's another more pressing task.

00:25:21.060 --> 00:25:26.030
The point was just putting it up and leaving it on the surface, so it wasn't doing properly.

00:25:26.030 --> 00:25:30.000
When would the Anderson have had to be built by?

00:25:30.280 --> 00:25:31.280
You know what?

00:25:31.280 --> 00:25:32.140
I don't know.

00:25:32.140 --> 00:25:33.140
You don't?

00:25:33.140 --> 00:25:34.140
I've got the blackout to finish.

00:25:34.140 --> 00:25:37.080
I think you should postpone the blackout till Monday.

00:25:37.080 --> 00:25:40.120
I think the blackout should be postponed.

00:25:40.120 --> 00:25:44.040
When Dad's finished digging in there, he doesn't want to come in here and start faffing about the blackout.

00:25:46.110 --> 00:25:49.240
I think Michael's panicking a bit now. He's got what he's always wanted.

00:25:49.240 --> 00:25:54.240
He's got the blackout blinds to do and the Anderson to make, so pressure's back on.

00:25:56.180 --> 00:26:00.000
And as we keep pointing out to him, he wanted to live in the 1940s, so...

00:26:00.000 --> 00:26:11.940
who he is. Realistically dig for several hours because none of us are fit. I would

00:26:11.940 --> 00:26:20.690
imagine people in 1940 were a lot fitter than we are now. Many wartime diaries

00:26:20.690 --> 00:26:24.670
recall how the task of building a shelter helped break down English

00:26:24.670 --> 00:26:29.670
Reserve. For many it was the first time they got to know their neighbors.

00:26:30.000 --> 00:26:31.240
Yeah.

00:26:31.240 --> 00:26:36.390
I'm not doing that for old Barry, not that.

00:26:36.390 --> 00:26:37.390
Yeah, we'll have a dig.

00:26:37.390 --> 00:26:39.240
Yeah.

00:26:39.240 --> 00:26:40.130
Righto.

00:26:40.130 --> 00:26:41.130
Shall we come through the garage?

00:26:41.130 --> 00:26:43.410
Alright.

00:26:43.410 --> 00:26:44.410
We'll see you shortly.

00:26:44.410 --> 00:26:46.610
How can you be called a wimp?

00:26:46.610 --> 00:26:48.080
Wimp?

00:26:48.080 --> 00:26:49.080
Look how far we've dug.

00:26:49.080 --> 00:26:50.080
How can you be called a wimp?

00:26:50.080 --> 00:26:51.190
Digging this lot.

00:26:51.190 --> 00:26:54.060
It's not something I would ask the strangers to do.

00:26:54.060 --> 00:26:59.070
I did feel that we did need help, although I do feel as though it's looked upon now as

00:26:59.070 --> 00:27:00.000
if we've sort of like...

00:27:00.160 --> 00:27:05.350
cheated in a way, but then again, I'm of the opinion that in the wartime, neighbours would

00:27:05.350 --> 00:27:09.490
all help each other. I know, OK, the neighbour offered to come in and help my dad dig the

00:27:09.490 --> 00:27:12.590
hole. I think that's what they would have done in the war, and although he hasn't gotten

00:27:12.590 --> 00:27:16.050
Anderson for us to return the favour and go, you know, building, we could offer him something

00:27:16.050 --> 00:27:20.110
else, like perhaps vegetables out of our garden or something, and I'm sure that's what people

00:27:20.110 --> 00:27:25.260
did. That's the rhubarb. That's all we've got ready in our garden, three pieces of rhubarb.

00:27:25.260 --> 00:27:30.000
So, I'm hoping we've got some fruit left that I can mix with it, but, hey, in the

00:27:30.000 --> 00:27:31.670
- No, we haven't.

00:27:31.670 --> 00:27:35.730
So we're having a very small rhubarb crumble with custard

00:27:35.730 --> 00:27:38.610
and a tray of scones for our neighbours

00:27:38.610 --> 00:27:42.720
who are kindly helping us dig the Anderson.

00:27:42.720 --> 00:27:44.660
- What does that mean, Kirstie?

00:27:44.660 --> 00:27:46.900
- It's just broken into pieces.

00:27:46.900 --> 00:27:49.100
- It's too dry, it means you need more...

00:27:49.100 --> 00:27:50.000
- Water.

00:27:50.000 --> 00:27:52.800
- Water or butter or lard or something in it.

00:27:52.800 --> 00:27:55.420
- I can't waste it, I've got to get it right.

00:27:55.420 --> 00:27:59.380
I mean, every 1940s housewife knew how to make basic pastry.

00:28:00.000 --> 00:28:02.970
- Everyone in war, the housewife included,

00:28:02.970 --> 00:28:05.440
was called upon to play their part.

00:28:05.440 --> 00:28:07.300
To control supplies and distribution,

00:28:07.300 --> 00:28:09.700
a minister of food was appointed.

00:28:09.700 --> 00:28:14.050
Lord Woolton was placed in charge of managing the kitchen.

00:28:14.050 --> 00:28:19.490
- Good evening, it is to you, the housewives of Britain,

00:28:19.910 --> 00:28:21.780
that I want to talk tonight.

00:28:21.780 --> 00:28:25.240
We have a job to do together, you and I,

00:28:25.240 --> 00:28:27.490
an immensely important war job.

00:28:27.490 --> 00:28:29.220
And I'm going to be very practical,

00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:36.500
and a bit personal too. Now, don't tell me that you never waste a thing.

00:28:36.500 --> 00:28:38.300
Sod it!

00:28:43.630 --> 00:28:48.630
Oh God, we've nothing else to eat. I knew this would bloody happen.

00:28:50.320 --> 00:28:51.510
What's that?

00:28:51.510 --> 00:28:52.870
Get out!

00:28:52.870 --> 00:28:53.870
What is that?

00:28:53.870 --> 00:29:00.000
It's the hole of the tea, the crumble, all the peaches, all our rhubarb out there lot, man. On the floor.

00:29:01.340 --> 00:29:05.500
All our flour, all our sugar, everything gone.

00:29:05.500 --> 00:29:08.270
Oh, the tarts are going to get it as well.

00:29:08.270 --> 00:29:14.230
Must make you really cross actually, spending all that time to drop it on the floor.

00:29:14.230 --> 00:29:16.900
I was really looking forward to that.

00:29:16.900 --> 00:29:19.220
There's absolutely nothing else for tea.

00:29:19.220 --> 00:29:21.160
So is it jam and bread again for tea?

00:29:21.160 --> 00:29:24.740
No, because I've used the jam.

00:29:24.740 --> 00:29:29.090
I thought we were going to have for tea because we haven't got anything else.

00:29:31.310 --> 00:29:32.210
Yeah?

00:29:32.210 --> 00:29:35.370
You've dropped the tea.

00:29:35.370 --> 00:29:39.600
Right, they should drop it or whip the flour over.

00:29:39.600 --> 00:29:41.600
No, so here's the jam in the tart.

00:29:41.600 --> 00:29:43.160
It's our favourite.

00:29:43.160 --> 00:29:47.130
I'm just wondering if I can salvage the rhubarb.

00:29:47.130 --> 00:29:50.130
You're not picking that up off the floor.

00:29:50.130 --> 00:29:51.130
Would you?

00:29:51.130 --> 00:29:52.130
Would you?

00:29:52.130 --> 00:29:54.130
If you were in war, proper war time.

00:29:54.130 --> 00:29:57.110
That's taken me all day to prepare.

00:29:57.110 --> 00:30:00.000
Oh no, I'm not eating it off the floor.

00:30:02.560 --> 00:30:08.720
do, we're going to have to swap roles.

00:30:08.720 --> 00:30:14.550
There may be difficult days ahead of us, far more difficult than we've seen yet, we shall

00:30:14.550 --> 00:30:15.550
still need feeding.

00:30:15.550 --> 00:30:18.380
Do you want me to clear the floor for you?

00:30:18.380 --> 00:30:19.380
No.

00:30:19.380 --> 00:30:23.480
Things are going to get tougher and I just hope I can cope with it.

00:30:23.480 --> 00:30:27.800
We have to get organised and especially as things start to go wrong, which I'm sure they

00:30:27.800 --> 00:30:29.670
will as the war progresses.

00:30:30.000 --> 00:30:31.300
I'm sorry.

00:30:31.700 --> 00:30:35.560
We're not eating very well because we're living on bread and jam.

00:30:35.780 --> 00:30:39.560
The only proper meal that we've had today was probably our Weetabix.

00:30:39.860 --> 00:30:42.520
That's the only thing apart from bread and jam that we've had.

00:30:44.420 --> 00:30:49.920
And we nearly had to have bread and jam again because Granny spilled all the food and tea.

00:30:50.100 --> 00:30:54.480
Yeah, but now we've got a casserole which it's going to take forever to cook.

00:30:55.360 --> 00:30:57.900
And if it is, we'll have tea at nine o'clock at night.

00:30:58.260 --> 00:31:00.000
It'll be past.

00:31:00.000 --> 00:31:02.920
I want to go home.

00:31:02.920 --> 00:31:05.720
I don't want to go home.

00:31:05.720 --> 00:31:30.000
Well, I do.

00:31:30.000 --> 00:31:33.670
The spring rains will flood the shelter Michael works on,

00:31:33.670 --> 00:31:39.220
despite the deadline of a blackout inspection that evening.

00:31:39.220 --> 00:31:43.970
Kirsty is anxious the War Cabinet may penalise them if they fail.

00:31:43.970 --> 00:31:47.570
I think we should fine them, actually, if they contravene blackout regulations.

00:31:47.570 --> 00:31:52.030
I think the social pressures are absolutely huge. People took the blackout very seriously.

00:31:52.030 --> 00:31:55.840
They thought that if a chink of light was showing or you lit a cigarette in the street,

00:31:55.840 --> 00:32:00.000
then you really were likely to bring the German bombs down on

00:32:00.000 --> 00:32:05.440
particular area, that particular house. The enforcement of these regulations was

00:32:05.440 --> 00:32:09.020
draconian. It's not just a matter of blacking out the windows, they have to

00:32:09.020 --> 00:32:15.520
make sure that every chink of light is stopped and 80% of middle-class

00:32:15.520 --> 00:32:20.390
households during the first weeks of the war failed in this task. So it's a

00:32:20.390 --> 00:32:23.760
really testing operation.

00:32:23.760 --> 00:32:26.040
Is your blackout really black?

00:32:26.220 --> 00:32:28.540
Can it be detested?

00:32:29.120 --> 00:32:30.000
Don't keep flying.

00:32:30.960 --> 00:32:40.080
your back go out and inspect it is your blackout really black have you drawn that curtain here's

00:32:40.080 --> 00:32:52.880
the warden on your track it's better to be certain is your blackout as we're starting to get more

00:32:52.880 --> 00:32:57.290
and more of the blackout material up at the windows I am beginning to feel as though we're

00:32:57.290 --> 00:33:00.000
going to feel trapped in and pending or whatever and I don't know how

00:33:00.000 --> 00:33:04.240
women coped with it. We've got a warden coming round in an hour

00:33:04.240 --> 00:33:08.840
and a half. What time is it now? Nearly six o'clock.

00:33:08.840 --> 00:33:13.130
And the house is not blacked out sufficiently. Right.

00:33:13.130 --> 00:33:15.770
I think it should be all hands on debt to get it blacked out because...

00:33:15.770 --> 00:33:19.950
What, no, I've got to feed the children. Well, it's coming up all hands on debt for

00:33:19.950 --> 00:33:25.210
half an hour. If the house isn't sufficiently blacked out, we're going to get really, really

00:33:25.210 --> 00:33:30.000
a hefty fine. Kirsty wants you to come and do a blackout.

00:33:30.120 --> 00:33:31.500
concentrate on the blackout now?

00:33:31.500 --> 00:33:34.180
No. Well, I'll need to get the change.

00:33:34.740 --> 00:33:36.380
In half an hour, then, we start blackout

00:33:36.380 --> 00:33:38.500
and I'll get the tea on, your father will get change,

00:33:38.800 --> 00:33:40.040
which will make it half past six.

00:33:40.440 --> 00:33:42.490
Well, I'll just carry on then. You're doing your best,

00:33:42.570 --> 00:33:43.690
but what? I'll be blacked out.

00:33:45.780 --> 00:33:47.160
Can you manage?

00:33:49.940 --> 00:33:52.180
I don't like it. It looks

00:33:52.180 --> 00:33:54.180
like a slum. I want to go

00:33:54.180 --> 00:33:56.420
home. It looks as if we're living in

00:33:56.420 --> 00:34:00.000
total squalor now. Can't make this

00:34:00.120 --> 00:34:03.390
Pretty. It's just getting darker by the minute.

00:34:03.390 --> 00:34:06.690
I'm going to have to go out why it's the light.

00:34:06.690 --> 00:34:09.170
What time is it?

00:34:09.170 --> 00:34:10.990
I'll say it's got around 10 minutes.

00:34:10.990 --> 00:34:14.410
It's been a hell of a day and it's all fallen to pieces a bit now.

00:34:14.410 --> 00:34:16.820
Kirstie's had enough.

00:34:16.820 --> 00:34:22.280
Michael's absolutely exhausted. I've done a load of washing and I'm really tired.

00:34:22.280 --> 00:34:25.550
I'm concerned about my family's health.

00:34:25.550 --> 00:34:28.870
My husband in particular is overweight and over 50.

00:34:30.220 --> 00:34:32.540
and he's had to work harder physically

00:34:32.540 --> 00:34:34.160
than he's had to work in a lot of years.

00:34:34.600 --> 00:34:37.060
And we're now waiting for a visit from the ARP ladies.

00:34:37.610 --> 00:34:39.050
Turn the light on, please.

00:34:45.420 --> 00:34:49.930
It took months for people to adapt

00:34:49.930 --> 00:34:52.570
to the claustrophobia blackout prompted in the home.

00:34:52.970 --> 00:34:56.100
But the fear of attracting bombers made people vigilant.

00:34:57.120 --> 00:35:00.000
In some cases, neighbours took to stoning the windows

00:35:00.000 --> 00:35:02.600
of homes that persisted in showing lights.

00:35:02.600 --> 00:35:08.420
We haven't had time to do it properly,

00:35:08.420 --> 00:35:10.180
so we'll probably get a fine.

00:35:10.180 --> 00:35:13.640
And, oh, that sounds like the air raid patrol.

00:35:13.640 --> 00:35:15.030
No.

00:35:15.030 --> 00:35:18.850
So it's nice and on the cake time today, really.

00:35:18.850 --> 00:35:24.290
But that's what we agreed, this is what we agreed to do.

00:35:24.290 --> 00:35:28.360
Hello.

00:35:28.360 --> 00:35:30.000
Making the inspection

00:35:30.120 --> 00:35:35.520
- Patricia Nolden, who at the age of 17 became West Wickham's youngest ARP warden.

00:35:35.520 --> 00:35:38.320
- I guess we'll be smiling if you fine us.

00:35:38.320 --> 00:35:40.420
- I don't do the fine. - Oh, right.

00:35:40.420 --> 00:35:42.520
- But I might put your name forward to the police.

00:35:42.520 --> 00:35:44.920
- Reading one of the books, I made this frame

00:35:44.920 --> 00:35:50.740
so that I can just lift it down during the day and let the light in.

00:35:50.740 --> 00:35:53.260
- And this... - That's a good idea.

00:35:53.260 --> 00:35:58.290
- Most important, that's not going to get any light through any changes, isn't it?

00:35:58.290 --> 00:36:00.000
- That's what you need to be up here. - Yeah.

00:36:00.000 --> 00:36:01.000
Yeah, that one's permanent.

00:36:01.000 --> 00:36:02.000
Oh, so that's all right.

00:36:02.000 --> 00:36:03.080
This is what I did.

00:36:03.080 --> 00:36:04.080
Yeah.

00:36:04.080 --> 00:36:05.060
Well, the brown paper's sticky stuck.

00:36:05.060 --> 00:36:05.970
Yeah.

00:36:05.970 --> 00:36:06.970
Well, that looks very efficient.

00:36:06.970 --> 00:36:10.370
It should be all right, shouldn't it?

00:36:10.370 --> 00:36:11.370
It's worth my work.

00:36:11.370 --> 00:36:12.550
Yes.

00:36:12.550 --> 00:36:13.990
What have you done about upstairs?

00:36:13.990 --> 00:36:14.990
The same as downstairs.

00:36:14.990 --> 00:36:15.990
It's the same.

00:36:15.990 --> 00:36:16.990
It's the same arrangement.

00:36:16.990 --> 00:36:17.990
In our bedroom.

00:36:17.990 --> 00:36:18.910
Yes.

00:36:18.910 --> 00:36:19.910
Yes, that's done.

00:36:19.910 --> 00:36:20.870
Well, I think you'll get away with it.

00:36:20.870 --> 00:36:23.790
We will keep an eye on you, of course.

