I've asked, but I haven't at the moment.

01:12:59.440 --> 01:13:00.000
Almond oil.

01:13:00.670 --> 01:13:03.240
- I've asked again, but I haven't got any... - Olive oil.

01:13:03.240 --> 01:13:06.760
- The same with the olive oil. - Cauliflower.

01:13:06.760 --> 01:13:12.570
- I've asked, but I haven't got any as yet.

01:13:12.570 --> 01:13:21.350
We are aware all the time of every single aspect of wartime economy.

01:13:21.350 --> 01:13:24.490
I have a tiny bit of cold cream left.

01:13:24.490 --> 01:13:29.250
I use it every other day, very sparingly.

01:13:29.250 --> 01:13:29.890
See?

01:13:30.160 --> 01:13:35.850
Toothpaste, we keep in a little egg cup, which we let the children use because we know we

01:13:35.850 --> 01:13:41.750
can't get any more toothpaste. The adults use just water or salt and water, whichever

01:13:41.750 --> 01:13:52.490
they prefer. We don't use toothpaste at all. We don't have shampoo. We wash our hair in

01:13:52.490 --> 01:14:00.000
just water and not as frequently as we would perhaps at home. The toilet roll, the toilet

01:14:00.510 --> 01:14:05.500
Now at home we'd normally have probably five toilet rolls a week.

01:14:05.500 --> 01:14:07.660
Here we use one a week.

01:14:07.660 --> 01:14:14.150
And this is because we are constantly aware of the fact that paper is in short supply.

01:14:14.150 --> 01:14:21.460
The soap liquid that's left on and around the sink I would use to wash my hands with.

01:14:21.460 --> 01:14:27.000
The washing powder's now gone and it's hard water so you never get anything other than scum.

01:14:27.000 --> 01:14:29.220
I haven't seen a bubble since I left home.

01:14:32.490 --> 01:14:49.460
"I don't want to be in the 1940s anymore. I want to go home. I want to go home. I want to smoke cigarettes. I want to eat decent food and rest. That's what I want to do."

01:14:49.460 --> 01:14:59.640
To add to the misery of having no cigarettes for the next few weeks, Kirstie has now dropped the lawnmower on her foot.

01:15:04.860 --> 01:15:08.330
Let's try the core compress, see if it fetches the bruising down.

01:15:08.330 --> 01:15:13.010
With nearly half the nation's doctors and nurses on military duty, there was less medical

01:15:13.010 --> 01:15:14.690
assistance for those left at home.

01:15:14.690 --> 01:15:16.740
Can't you just keep your leg up for, say, 20 minutes?

01:15:16.740 --> 01:15:18.760
Hmm.

01:15:18.760 --> 01:15:20.320
I don't think there's much else we can do, really.

01:15:20.320 --> 01:15:24.690
But my big toe, I just can't feel my... you know when something feels really numb?

01:15:24.690 --> 01:15:27.820
Kirstie's bruises, though painful, should soon heal.

01:15:27.820 --> 01:15:28.820
It dropped.

01:15:28.820 --> 01:15:30.000
You know, as it went down...

01:15:30.000 --> 01:15:34.990
it from a great height or whatever, but I dropped, dropped, and rolled.

01:15:35.650 --> 01:15:40.710
We can't have them sitting around feeling sorry for themselves at a time when everyone

01:15:40.710 --> 01:15:42.170
was being urged to do their bit.

01:15:42.590 --> 01:15:45.430
Well, what they could do, of course, is to do some voluntary work.

01:15:45.730 --> 01:15:49.890
I mean, over a million women joined the Women's Voluntary Service, the WVS.

01:15:50.200 --> 01:15:55.250
They escorted evacuees, they ran nurseries, they ran mobile canteens at bomb incidents.

01:15:55.750 --> 01:16:00.000
Don't forget, they really were in the front line during the Second World War, nearly 250

01:16:00.030 --> 01:16:06.760
members of the WVS were killed and their motto was if it needs doing the WVS will do it.

01:16:35.760 --> 01:16:43.200
Dear Mrs Hymers, the Women's Voluntary Service was founded in 1938 to help the war effort.

01:16:43.910 --> 01:16:47.150
Members of the local community will be in touch with you shortly

01:16:47.150 --> 01:16:49.910
to advise you of the jobs that we have chosen for you.

01:16:53.180 --> 01:16:59.990
I feel sick when I think about the work we're going to have to do in the Voluntary Service.

01:17:00.000 --> 01:17:06.550
services. These days, in the year 2000, you know exactly what you're going to do when

01:17:06.550 --> 01:17:12.610
you apply for a job. You know, to a certain extent, what the job entails. We have no conception

01:17:12.610 --> 01:17:17.210
whatsoever of what we're going to be asked to do in the voluntary services, and that

01:17:17.210 --> 01:17:22.390
makes me feel really apprehensive. Our war cabinet have found Lyn and Kirsty

01:17:22.390 --> 01:17:27.840
voluntary jobs, similar to those that were carried out by the WVS.

01:17:34.350 --> 01:17:36.720
Hello. Hello, my name's Siobhan Ross.

01:17:36.720 --> 01:17:39.120
I've come from St. Mark's Nursery Group,

01:17:39.120 --> 01:17:41.270
and the Cabinet have given me your name

01:17:41.270 --> 01:17:43.570
as being interested in doing some voluntary work.

01:17:43.570 --> 01:17:45.070
Oh, do you want to come through?

01:17:45.070 --> 01:17:47.620
Yes, if that's convenient.

01:17:47.620 --> 01:17:48.720
Come on in.

01:17:48.720 --> 01:17:54.700
I was just explaining to your daughter,

01:17:54.700 --> 01:17:56.700
I've come from St. Mark's Nursery Group.

01:17:56.700 --> 01:17:57.600
Oh.

01:17:57.600 --> 01:17:59.640
Your name has been given to me

01:18:00.000 --> 01:18:05.290
cabinet. They've said that you were interested in voluntary work. Can I interest you in coming

01:18:05.290 --> 01:18:11.500
to work at the nursery group? With either of you, I'd welcome any help whatsoever.

01:18:11.500 --> 01:18:16.190
I'm quite happy to do that, but obviously I cannot give you a definite decision now

01:18:16.190 --> 01:18:19.970
at this present time about tomorrow. Right. Because I don't know how I'm going to feel

01:18:19.970 --> 01:18:23.870
in the morning. No, that's all right. But I'm quite happy to do that. You're quite

01:18:23.870 --> 01:18:30.000
happy. They're all fairly clean children. We haven't got any head lice at the moment.

01:18:30.000 --> 01:18:42.570
I'm from... I've been asked to call only by the Cabinet Office to discuss some voluntary

01:18:42.570 --> 01:18:43.570
work.

01:18:43.570 --> 01:18:47.930
Oh, more. I'm your woman. I'm your woman. Come in. Walk this way.

01:18:47.930 --> 01:18:53.110
While the wartime WVS collected for families who'd been bombed out, Lynn and Kirstie will

01:18:53.110 --> 01:18:54.890
collect for the local charity shop.

01:18:54.890 --> 01:18:57.640
How do you think you could collect the sacks? Have you got anything?

01:18:57.640 --> 01:19:00.000
Well, my daughter's got a little truck for her bank.

01:19:00.000 --> 01:19:05.550
which you can't poem, so don't know how that one will go, but I shall work round that one.

01:19:05.550 --> 01:19:09.930
We'll just do what we can for you. That's all I can say at this moment in time.

01:19:09.930 --> 01:19:20.160
Excellent. Bye-bye. Thank you.

01:19:20.160 --> 01:19:23.160
Who's going to go to Lovegroves? Who's going to take the kids to school?

01:19:23.160 --> 01:19:30.000
Who's going to do the washing? Who's going to do the 101 things that need doing in a 1940s

01:19:30.000 --> 01:19:48.900
part of me wants to go home, part of me wants to stay and do better, because I feel that

01:19:48.900 --> 01:19:53.420
there's been some failure here. I've got to get into a more positive frame of mind and

01:19:53.420 --> 01:20:00.000
at the moment I'm struggling with that. I think I'll frighten children.

01:20:00.350 --> 01:20:02.650
It's a bit stern. It's drinked.

01:20:02.650 --> 01:20:07.300
Over 1400 state nurseries were set up to free women for war work.

01:20:07.300 --> 01:20:11.980
A mother could leave her child before breakfast and collect it after supper,

01:20:11.980 --> 01:20:17.780
knowing that it would have balanced meals as well as free milk, juice and rosehip syrup.

01:20:22.720 --> 01:20:27.940
Women were encouraged to have more children and pregnant mothers were given extra rations.

01:20:27.940 --> 01:20:30.000
The birth rate went up by

01:20:30.000 --> 01:20:30.940
22%.

01:20:30.940 --> 01:20:38.420
Some doctors even advised having babies as a cure for wartime anxiety.

01:20:48.200 --> 01:20:49.620
I'm resisting it.

01:20:49.760 --> 01:20:49.940
What?

01:20:50.260 --> 01:20:51.800
You're putting your knickers in here, aren't you?

01:20:52.210 --> 01:20:57.300
Outside the house, Lynn and Kirsty are exposed to the temptations of modern living.

01:21:01.020 --> 01:21:05.340
In wartime, this cake would have used all their points rations for at least two months.

01:21:06.470 --> 01:21:09.640
What's more, it was illegal for bakers to use icing.

01:21:11.090 --> 01:21:15.340
Ooh, that's got a coffee as well, you know.

01:21:16.720 --> 01:21:17.790
Don't mention the name.

01:21:19.450 --> 01:21:29.950
My relationship with Kirsty has changed a great deal over the last week.

01:21:30.800 --> 01:21:36.580
Kirstie and I have always argued, normally I'm the stronger one, but the last week has

01:21:36.580 --> 01:21:37.860
sort of turned that on its head.

01:21:38.350 --> 01:21:44.500
Kirstie has become even stronger than she was before and has almost, in a sense, looked

01:21:44.500 --> 01:21:45.120
after me.

01:21:45.810 --> 01:21:50.500
The turning point for me was when we had such a bad couple of days, we didn't have the cigarettes

01:21:50.500 --> 01:21:52.280
and one thing or another, we both got very down.

01:21:53.140 --> 01:21:56.040
For the first time ever, my dad was not around for my mum.

01:21:56.280 --> 01:22:00.000
I felt like it was my place that I had to do something for my mum or whatever.

01:22:00.000 --> 01:22:04.700
and for myself. And I felt as though I had to be the stronger person, and I just felt

01:22:04.700 --> 01:22:08.200
like we would just go round in circles if somebody doesn't pull themselves together

01:22:08.200 --> 01:22:11.960
and sort things out, then we don't need to feel like complete failures, do we?

01:22:12.020 --> 01:22:18.920
We must all stick together, all stick together, and the clouds will soon roll by.

01:22:19.720 --> 01:22:24.940
I think collecting the scope bags is a worthwhile experience because at the end of the day it

01:22:24.940 --> 01:22:30.000
benefiting somebody else I don't mind the hard physical

01:22:30.000 --> 01:22:36.100
work because women in the 40s would have done physical hard work.

01:22:36.100 --> 01:22:45.240
During the war, 241 members died while on active duty. So whatever we are asked to do,

01:22:45.240 --> 01:23:00.000
we know that we have to do it because we'd be letting the WVS down.

01:23:00.000 --> 01:23:18.800
is definitely a duty when you look your best you feel self-confident and your

01:23:18.800 --> 01:23:24.210
confidence transmits itself to those around you while beauty for duty became

01:23:24.210 --> 01:23:30.000
a popular wartime slogan the challenge for women was to be beautiful without cosmetics

01:23:30.730 --> 01:23:35.550
I personally think the 1940s woman would hold on to the make-up till the bitter end.

01:23:35.550 --> 01:23:38.250
Lipstick was supposed to be worn every day.

01:23:38.250 --> 01:23:41.250
It was called the Red Badge of Courage and worn throughout the war.

01:23:41.250 --> 01:23:47.440
From 1941 onwards, it became virtually illegal to manufacture hair and beauty products.

01:23:47.440 --> 01:23:52.330
This only encouraged women to turn to the black market.

01:23:52.330 --> 01:23:57.080
Even legitimate shops sell nail varnish disguised as ladder stop.

01:24:00.000 --> 01:24:06.320
In all aspects of beauty and feminine hygiene, the wartime woman was forced to improvise.

01:24:06.320 --> 01:24:12.280
I have become desperate in the last few days because my white roots are showing through

01:24:12.280 --> 01:24:13.280
all my hair.

01:24:13.280 --> 01:24:18.040
And I thought a vegetable dye would be the best way forward, so I'm finding all the things

01:24:18.040 --> 01:24:23.620
from the garden that I know create some sort of stain, and really making it up as I go

01:24:23.620 --> 01:24:25.120
along.

01:24:25.120 --> 01:24:26.120
It stinks.

01:24:26.120 --> 01:24:28.290
How's it looking?

01:24:30.570 --> 01:24:31.420
It's dark.

01:24:31.420 --> 01:24:33.200
Yeah?

01:24:33.200 --> 01:24:35.190
Are you just saying that to make me happy?

01:24:35.190 --> 01:24:36.890
No, it's...

01:24:36.890 --> 01:24:37.810
I hope when you go out it doesn't rain.

01:24:37.810 --> 01:24:38.940
Oh, Kirsty!

01:24:38.940 --> 01:24:40.920
It's all over me!

01:24:40.920 --> 01:24:47.700
When I put on that uniform, I change.

01:24:47.700 --> 01:24:50.800
I become Mrs. Organised.

01:24:50.800 --> 01:24:51.950
Mrs. Wonderful.

01:24:51.950 --> 01:24:54.220
Mrs. Can cope with anything.

01:24:54.220 --> 01:24:55.380
Mrs. Will cope with anything.

01:24:55.380 --> 01:25:00.000
We'll push boundaries further.

01:25:00.000 --> 01:25:06.580
the uniform is doing for me what prozac does for thousands of women in in the year 2000

01:25:12.000 --> 01:25:17.720
morale is up and lynn and kirsty have decided they could be doing more for their war effort

01:25:17.720 --> 01:25:22.880
now they've taken matters into their own hands they're giving up their nursery school duties and

01:25:22.880 --> 01:25:30.000
have chosen instead to go and work in a local retirement home i thought working with older people

01:25:30.000 --> 01:25:34.830
actually be better for me because it would enable me to give something back to the wartime

01:25:34.830 --> 01:25:40.850
generation that did suffer so much and I think they'll enjoy perhaps the uniform and to chat

01:25:40.850 --> 01:25:46.500
about what we're doing and I will feel more useful and more really in keeping with what

01:25:46.500 --> 01:25:50.710
a WVS lady would have tried to do throughout the war.

01:26:08.250 --> 01:26:16.250
Is it right? Does it look? I don't know.

01:26:16.250 --> 01:26:20.140
Does it look right?

01:26:20.140 --> 01:26:26.810
Shortages are hurting, you know, there's no soap, shampoo.

01:26:26.810 --> 01:26:29.730
That's why her hair is like this.

01:26:31.660 --> 01:26:33.660
What did you use? Just water?

01:26:33.660 --> 01:26:35.660
Oh, no, just soap.

01:26:35.660 --> 01:26:37.660
Oh, no, I never know.

01:26:37.660 --> 01:26:39.660
Oh, no, we weren't favourite.

01:26:39.660 --> 01:26:41.660
We weren't that well off, it was soap.

01:26:41.660 --> 01:26:44.660
I hope you're only having five inches of water in your bath.

01:26:44.660 --> 01:26:46.670
We certainly are, we've got a black line round.

01:26:46.670 --> 01:26:48.670
What, is that the tide mark?

01:26:48.670 --> 01:26:50.670
We've got black lines round the black line,

01:26:50.670 --> 01:26:52.670
because there's nothing to clean the bath with.

01:26:54.670 --> 01:26:58.670
When the older generation says that the younger generation

01:26:58.670 --> 01:27:00.000
that were better off than they were,

01:27:00.000 --> 01:27:04.360
and I really do believe that, having listened to some of the experiences

01:27:04.360 --> 01:27:07.560
and having to do some of the physical hard work that I've had to do,

01:27:08.040 --> 01:27:12.050
I have got a lot of admiration for the people that lived through the war.

01:27:13.480 --> 01:27:18.660
When I'd cleared the plates from the dining tables,

01:27:19.130 --> 01:27:23.690
the food I was putting into the waste bin would have fed my family for a few days.

01:27:24.190 --> 01:27:27.790
We're at home in 2000, I'm the most wasteful woman in the world,

01:27:27.790 --> 01:27:30.000
but here I can't bear to waste anything.

01:27:30.000 --> 01:27:38.880
It was actually illegal to waste things, and you were not supposed to throw away any paper.

01:27:38.880 --> 01:27:43.000
Paper had to be reused, and it said that at the bottom of every magazine.

01:27:43.000 --> 01:27:48.320
You were not supposed to feed your pets anything that was possible for human consumption.

01:27:48.320 --> 01:27:54.320
And indeed, amiss that Bridget O'Sullivan, Barnet in Hertfordshire in 1942, she was fined

01:27:54.320 --> 01:27:58.930
ten pounds worth two guineas costs for feeding bread to the birds.

01:28:00.960 --> 01:28:06.370
As you can see, the National Loaf, as it's usual, fresh and spongy self, not.

01:28:06.370 --> 01:28:13.680
Their loaf is bread made from National Wheatmeal flour, which had calcium and vitamins added.

01:28:13.680 --> 01:28:21.510
It is horrible, it is dry, and it's got really, really hard crusts, and it's very great.

01:28:21.510 --> 01:28:24.440
I also find it really difficult to keep it fresh.

01:28:24.440 --> 01:28:30.000
The National Loaf was so unpopular in its day, it became known

01:28:30.000 --> 01:28:35.190
as Hitler's secret weapon and the government was believed to have put out

01:28:35.190 --> 01:28:39.250
the rumor that it had aphrodisiac properties to encourage the nation to

01:28:39.250 --> 01:28:58.550
eat more. Hang on, it's not right. The bread's not right. It's not just stale, it smells foisty and I know it's not bad but I'm hungry.

01:29:08.350 --> 01:29:29.900
Lovegrove, my grocer, thinks that the worst thing in the world that can happen to him is me going in his shop, him not being able to supply cigarettes. But I have to tell you, he's wrong. Because what he hasn't yet seen is me expecting important visitors.

01:29:30.350 --> 01:29:41.710
and having no vim. Marguerite Patton wrote to my mum and said she'd like to come for

01:29:41.710 --> 01:29:47.450
tea. As I've sort of adapted the role of being the baker, I've got to somehow make some nice

01:29:47.450 --> 01:29:51.300
cakes and nice things for her to eat, which is beginning to stress me out a little bit

01:29:51.300 --> 01:29:56.320
because I'm not quite sure what to make. Kirstie has every reason to be anxious, as

01:29:56.320 --> 01:30:00.000
Marguerite Patton was the most famous cook of her day. She taught me how to eat. She taught

01:30:00.000 --> 01:30:07.400
Alternation, how to make do with cunning recipes for mock apricot tarts and honey made from boiled parsley.

01:30:08.000 --> 01:30:15.270
It's a nightmare. I've been awake all night. I'm really nervous. I've come out in a nervous rush.

01:30:19.700 --> 01:30:28.970
So, I mean, I am enjoying doing it, but I just, it's the thought of, you know, Margaret Patton eating my food.

01:30:30.320 --> 01:30:34.420
it'll probably all turn out wrong and I'll probably burn it all. But if I burn it, I'll

01:30:34.420 --> 01:30:40.690
have to put that on a plate because I won't have anything else. Under normal circumstances,

01:30:40.690 --> 01:30:46.420
I would clean this house and polish it from top to bottom if I was having such an important

01:30:46.420 --> 01:30:51.700
visitor as Marguerite. But because of the wall work I'm doing, priorities change, everything

01:30:51.700 --> 01:31:00.000
changes. I'm making a chocolate cake and I'm using the children's cake, which is normally

01:31:00.000 --> 01:31:22.100
to make um eggy bread i feel incredibly guilty but there you go lynn's first priority is now

01:31:22.100 --> 01:31:30.000
her voluntary work kirstie's left to do all the catering on her own kirstie's

01:31:30.030 --> 01:31:33.550
struggling to make do with what's available but the sausage meat doesn't

01:31:33.550 --> 01:31:38.130
look promising the sausage meat boiling them in a pan look absolutely disgusting

01:31:38.130 --> 01:31:43.600
and I really won't have to serve that to anybody at the nursing home Lynn now

01:31:43.600 --> 01:31:50.240
visits daily she has made friends with wartime fireman Reginald Long Reg was

01:31:50.240 --> 01:31:55.660
keen for her to know of some of the unsung heroes of the home front I was on

01:31:55.660 --> 01:32:00.000
leave it was my wedding anniversary and they were killed on a Sunday night

01:32:00.000 --> 01:32:06.220
you see of course I go back Monday morning and the wife had made a lovely cake for the boys at the

01:32:06.220 --> 01:32:11.410
station and when I got to the gate I knew there was something wrong you see and I said to Reggie

01:32:11.410 --> 01:32:18.520
Nell what's going wrong he said something very serious and I knew then so of course the cake

01:32:18.520 --> 01:32:25.640
went home again and what had happened to your friends well they they were at an assembly point

01:32:25.640 --> 01:32:30.000
and you had to go to an assembly point and then they dispatched it

01:32:30.000 --> 01:32:31.850
crews from that assembly

01:32:31.850 --> 01:32:33.770
point to wherever you were required.

01:32:34.910 --> 01:32:36.510
Well, this crew, there was

01:32:36.510 --> 01:32:38.450
about three crews there, and

01:32:38.450 --> 01:32:40.410
they got a direct hitch, you see, before they were

01:32:40.410 --> 01:32:44.220
dispersed. And how many were killed,

01:32:44.340 --> 01:32:45.100
Reg? 21.

01:32:45.760 --> 01:32:48.280
In one night, 21 beckoning boys

01:32:48.280 --> 01:32:49.240
in one night.

01:33:06.360 --> 01:33:11.160
have we got to complain about because we can't get milk or shampoo or soap or

01:33:11.160 --> 01:33:15.720
washing powder or cigarettes even what does it matter we don't have death we

01:33:15.720 --> 01:33:20.710
don't have fear for our lives so the least we can do is fulfill the other

01:33:20.710 --> 01:33:24.970
bits of it we owe it to the people who did suffer and did struggle to do the

01:33:24.970 --> 01:33:26.470
best we can

01:33:36.300 --> 01:33:41.430
We've beaten our fuel target by 13 units, which is pretty good.

01:33:41.430 --> 01:33:48.550
And we beat it by 2 units, and that's like 15 units that we've saved.

01:33:48.810 --> 01:33:51.580
So that is quite a big achievement for me, really.

01:33:52.310 --> 01:33:54.440
And I'm pretty happy with it this week.

01:34:00.120 --> 01:34:06.880
When they first arrived seven weeks ago, it was Marguerite Patton who introduced the family to the 1940s house.

01:34:06.880 --> 01:34:09.970
Well, I'm very much looking forward to seeing the family again.

01:34:09.970 --> 01:34:16.730
I liked them so much when I met them briefly, and I'm longing to hear how they're getting on, how they've met the challenge.

01:34:16.730 --> 01:34:19.580
Hello, Lynn! How lovely to see you again!

01:34:19.580 --> 01:34:21.280
Oh, honey!

01:34:21.280 --> 01:34:23.080
And tomatoes.

01:34:23.080 --> 01:34:26.090
You must be ready for a cup of tea after all your hard work.

01:34:26.090 --> 01:34:29.670
Lovely. I say, haven't you been busy?

01:34:30.160 --> 01:34:32.950
