01:54:20.830 --> 01:54:30.000
When I was in the war, I never thought about my aches and pains.

01:54:30.000 --> 01:54:38.620
I'd just get up and think, "Ooh, my back hurts," and then I'd just get on with the day and forget all about it.

01:54:38.620 --> 01:55:00.000
We were working to the bum. We were working so hard, we were working our socks off.

01:55:00.000 --> 01:55:04.270
I just throw the washing in the washing machine, but as a wartime housewife

01:55:04.270 --> 01:55:10.450
I used to get up at 6 o'clock and it would often be midnight before the last sheet was on the bed.

01:55:10.450 --> 01:55:16.860
I don't know how she managed to get through it without having a heart attack or something,

01:55:16.860 --> 01:55:21.410
but she did it and I'm proud of her for doing it, coping with everything.

01:55:22.570 --> 01:55:30.000
We wanted to find out how nine weeks of wartime living would affect the health

01:55:30.060 --> 01:55:37.500
of the Heimers family. Before they began the adults underwent a series of rigorous tests.

01:55:37.500 --> 01:55:44.090
These were repeated when the Heimers war was over.

01:55:44.090 --> 01:55:48.930
The results showed that all the adults in the house had lost weight and our more detailed

01:55:48.930 --> 01:55:54.570
tests revealed this was all body fat. In fact they preserved their muscle tissue. They were

01:55:54.570 --> 01:56:00.000
physically fitter. This was confirmed when we saw that their blood pressure

01:56:00.000 --> 01:56:02.880
and their resting heart rate had come down too,

01:56:02.880 --> 01:56:06.000
suggesting that they were much healthier at the end of the study

01:56:06.000 --> 01:56:09.230
than when they first went into the house.

01:56:09.230 --> 01:56:13.650
Why was everything about me physically better

01:56:13.650 --> 01:56:19.320
when I was on this totally mundane and uninteresting diet,

01:56:19.320 --> 01:56:22.680
which I didn't feel was giving me the right nutrients?

01:56:22.680 --> 01:56:24.800
The health and well-being of many people

01:56:24.800 --> 01:56:27.970
did improve during the war, in spite of the shortages.

01:56:27.970 --> 01:56:30.000
Yes, and what we often forget is that

01:56:30.000 --> 01:56:36.180
the war, only half the population had enough income to get themselves a healthy diet, whereas

01:56:36.180 --> 01:56:47.290
when the war occurred, unemployment disappeared, there was lots of overtime. Men in industry often

01:56:47.290 --> 01:57:00.000
worked away from home. So Michael came back to work in Otley as part of his wartime experience. He's head of production at an

01:57:00.000 --> 01:57:05.340
engineering factory that makes parts for aircraft a job he might well have done

01:57:05.340 --> 01:57:11.700
in the war. During the war there was a large Abro factory just above Otley where

01:57:11.700 --> 01:57:16.480
they built Lancasters amongst other things and Lynn's father actually worked

01:57:16.480 --> 01:57:23.840
at that factory on the Abro Lancasters and that's how he came to Otley. When he was back in Otley

01:57:23.840 --> 01:57:30.000
on war work the Heimer's neighbour Sarah Drake cooked all Michael's meals based on authentic

01:57:30.000 --> 01:57:37.710
recipes of the day. I used to be Michael's British restaurant and he came

01:57:37.710 --> 01:57:46.010
home at 7 o'clock and he had things like sausage meatloaf, stew, dumplings, stuffed

01:57:46.010 --> 01:57:51.370
cabbage. During the war British restaurants, as they were called, sprung

01:57:51.370 --> 01:57:56.590
up all over the country. They offered nourishing three-course meals for 11

01:57:56.590 --> 01:58:00.000
pence, one pound fifty in today's money.

01:58:02.270 --> 01:58:05.950
No question of Michael going hungry.

01:58:05.950 --> 01:58:12.500
Ironically, although the Heimers found their diet unappetising and boring, they were getting

01:58:12.500 --> 01:58:17.750
all the nutrients they needed. They had much less fat and they were cooking proper meals

01:58:17.750 --> 01:58:21.090
rather than relying heavily on snacks and processed foods.

01:58:21.090 --> 01:58:25.890
So we can reassure those viewers who kindly sent in food parcels, the Heimers family was

01:58:25.890 --> 01:58:30.000
definitely not starving. The fact that some food parcels

01:58:30.000 --> 01:58:35.840
were sent to the family, because viewers felt they really were starving, is wonderful.

01:58:35.840 --> 01:58:40.600
And it is typical of what happened during the real war years.

01:58:40.600 --> 01:58:46.060
Whether you were rich or poor, you were facing the same common danger, and we all cared about

01:58:46.060 --> 01:58:49.740
each other, how we were faring, were we ill, were we well.

01:58:49.740 --> 01:58:55.570
There was a time when I just thought, I can't do this, I cannot do this anymore.

01:58:55.570 --> 01:58:59.530
Physically I was exhausted, mentally I was all over the place.

01:59:00.090 --> 01:59:05.940
and I felt so lonely, so terribly, terribly isolated.

01:59:05.940 --> 01:59:15.270
It is so hot, the washing and the ironing, daylight today, I don't know how they did it.

01:59:15.270 --> 01:59:23.090
I am absolutely wringing wet, seven o'clock, just got my air raid shelter we've been in

01:59:23.090 --> 01:59:26.950
since four, twelve o'clock last night, I'm tired.

01:59:40.400 --> 01:59:46.770
I felt sorry for them because they were looking so very tired and Peter decided they needed

01:59:46.770 --> 01:59:51.460
some firewood so made a bundle of sticks and did it as it would have been with the wire

01:59:51.460 --> 01:59:58.390
wrapped around it and we took it down and they seemed to accept it very gratefully.

02:00:00.000 --> 02:00:05.240
Mr. and Mrs. Tilling knocked on the door and Mrs. Tilling said,

02:00:06.230 --> 02:00:09.650
I know how hard it is for you and we're going to be your wartime neighbours.

02:00:10.580 --> 02:00:15.110
They came in, had a cup of tea and told us about their real wartime experiences.

02:00:15.400 --> 02:00:19.890
Nothing to do with our pretend war, but real wartime experiences.

02:00:22.390 --> 02:00:29.410
She just needed a bit of comfort and to have her confidence boosted a bit that she was doing well.

02:00:30.510 --> 02:00:35.030
and she was doing a very good job in the circumstances.

02:00:35.030 --> 02:00:40.360
She gave me a big hug, that's the best hug anybody's ever given me because the hug was

02:00:40.360 --> 02:00:45.380
what I needed more than anything else in the world, more than sticks, more than food, more

02:00:45.380 --> 02:00:46.380
than cigarettes.

02:00:46.380 --> 02:00:53.370
I needed that hug from Mrs Tilling and it just turned the whole thing round and I never,

02:00:53.370 --> 02:01:00.000
never felt like throwing in the towel again.

02:01:00.000 --> 02:01:03.000
You stood there very nonchalant, aren't you? Hand in the pocket.

02:01:03.000 --> 02:01:04.000
May I say, yeah.

02:01:04.000 --> 02:01:05.000
You can tell it's you.

02:01:05.000 --> 02:01:06.000
Yeah, we said that.

02:01:06.000 --> 02:01:11.010
Peter Tilling moved to Braemar Gardens in 1932.

02:01:11.010 --> 02:01:16.450
He was just 13 when the first bomb dropped on the street.

02:01:16.450 --> 02:01:24.160
It was only about a £150 bomb, but it did a lot of damage because it was between the two houses.

02:01:24.160 --> 02:01:26.160
So it actually landed behind...?

02:01:26.160 --> 02:01:28.160
It landed on the side wall of this house.

02:01:28.160 --> 02:01:29.220
Right.

02:01:38.510 --> 02:01:44.490
Only seven miles from Braemar Gardens, Chislehurst Caves was a refuge for families sheltering

02:01:44.490 --> 02:01:52.440
from Hitler's bombs.

02:01:52.440 --> 02:01:58.990
Night after night, in the darkest hours of the war, 22 miles of disused limestone mines

02:01:58.990 --> 02:02:00.000
became home to

02:02:00.000 --> 02:02:01.860
15,000 people.

02:02:11.750 --> 02:02:17.890
I found Chisselaer Caves very interesting because I thought out of the whole experience it was the

02:02:17.890 --> 02:02:24.290
one thing that brought me the closest to how people in the 1940s really lived and how they felt.

02:02:24.290 --> 02:02:29.730
So you could get them in their own position to operate.

02:02:31.050 --> 02:02:35.290
Chislehurst Caves tells a story of how the wartime generation

02:02:35.290 --> 02:02:43.690
pulled together. Here a thriving community built its own hospital,

02:02:43.690 --> 02:02:46.980
concert hall, hairdressers, canteen, church

02:02:46.980 --> 02:02:56.670
and even a cinema. The long tunnels

02:02:56.670 --> 02:03:00.000
of the caves were used to just house

02:03:00.000 --> 02:03:08.510
bunks which were two or three tiers high I believe. How did they keep the children occupied

02:03:08.510 --> 02:03:12.170
all night? How did they keep them quiet when there were several thousand people coming

02:03:12.170 --> 02:03:17.230
and going? How did they manage to get their sleep? How did they stop the children from

02:03:17.230 --> 02:03:18.230
being frightened?

02:03:18.230 --> 02:03:24.430
When I went in there and I saw all that I thought to myself I wouldn't have wanted

02:03:24.430 --> 02:03:30.000
to be living in the world. It really did show the hardships in the

02:03:30.000 --> 02:03:36.400
40s housewife, protecting her family. And I find that to be one of the greatest stories

02:03:36.400 --> 02:03:50.510
of the home front. So why don't we know about it? There's this strange divide between the

02:03:50.510 --> 02:03:54.730
reality of war, the reality of war on the home front, and our perception of it as told

02:03:54.730 --> 02:04:00.000
by our parents. I don't think the true horrors of war have

02:04:00.000 --> 02:04:05.010
really been passed on to my generation, not the war on the home front.

02:04:05.010 --> 02:04:09.690
The more the experience affected Lyn, the more she became concerned that those who suffered

02:04:09.690 --> 02:04:16.580
on the home front got little help after the war. She discussed this with a friend she

02:04:16.580 --> 02:04:22.370
visited every day at the local nursing home, wartime farmman Reg Long.

02:04:22.370 --> 02:04:26.290
My house was bombed and my wife and baby were trapped under the stairs.

02:04:26.290 --> 02:04:30.000
I didn't know that. They got them out all right. The house was in a bit of a mess.

02:04:30.030 --> 02:04:33.850
So you must have been frightened when you opened the front door, or when you got sore

02:04:33.850 --> 02:04:34.850
in your house.

02:04:34.850 --> 02:04:35.850
Well, not any front door.

02:04:35.850 --> 02:04:36.770
No, no, sir.

02:04:36.770 --> 02:04:37.770
To open.

02:04:37.770 --> 02:04:40.470
Nowadays, counselling is available.

02:04:40.470 --> 02:04:46.470
Reg himself thinks this is one of the good things about the later part of the 20th century,

02:04:46.470 --> 02:04:51.980
that people can get the help that they need if they're witnesses to horrendous events,

02:04:51.980 --> 02:04:53.280
but in those days they couldn't.

02:04:53.280 --> 02:04:54.280
I can't.

02:04:54.280 --> 02:04:56.400
I mean, there must have been some grim times for you at that.

02:04:56.400 --> 02:04:59.720
We had some very grim times, some that I wouldn't even...

02:05:00.000 --> 02:05:07.130
to you. In one of my conversations with Reg I learned that not everybody could

02:05:07.130 --> 02:05:13.210
cope. There were quite a few suicides. That's something that never occurred to me.

02:05:13.210 --> 02:05:21.400
Many people who suffered great tragedies and an awful experiences during the war

02:05:21.400 --> 02:05:24.950
had a period where they didn't want to talk about it. They just wanted to forget

02:05:24.950 --> 02:05:30.000
it, keep it to themselves. And then as the years went on they were ready

02:05:30.000 --> 02:05:35.040
to talk about it again they were ready for people to listen and then there was nobody really around

02:05:35.680 --> 02:05:46.570
who wanted to listen back at home in otley lynn continues to make friends with and talk to people

02:05:46.570 --> 02:05:54.360
of the wartime generation my new friend olive has been helping me

02:05:54.890 --> 02:06:00.000
rediscover things about my own past and how the war affected

02:06:00.000 --> 02:06:10.290
people in in my own town that's my father before he lost his hair oh yes of course it is yes yes

02:06:10.290 --> 02:06:22.860
my mother she's always immaculately dressed she perhaps you know perhaps about it I don't feel

02:06:22.860 --> 02:06:30.000
that my generation late 50s 60s we didn't want to know about the war wasn't anything to do

02:06:30.000 --> 02:06:47.330
with us. We were the new generation of teenagers, of young people.

02:06:47.330 --> 02:06:52.180
Later generations of parents are better at relationships with their children. They're

02:06:52.180 --> 02:07:00.000
able to talk more freely and openly. They have more in common. My generation couldn't

02:07:00.000 --> 02:07:30.000
have anything in common with a generation that had been through a world war.

02:07:30.000 --> 02:07:41.870
As a family, I do think from coming back from the 1940s experience we have all changed.

02:07:41.870 --> 02:07:46.460
I think myself and the children have got more confidence.

02:07:46.460 --> 02:07:54.180
I am a single mom and my husband left me and that affected me for, you know, quite a while.

02:07:54.180 --> 02:07:56.760
It's still something in me that I do lack in confidence.

02:08:01.210 --> 02:08:06.570
Kirstie, she has changed, she's become far, far more independent, she's her own woman,

02:08:06.570 --> 02:08:08.910
she's her own family unit.

02:08:08.910 --> 02:08:12.790
She seems to be coping with anything life throws at her at the moment, it's been very

02:08:12.790 --> 02:08:13.790
good for Kirstie.

02:08:13.790 --> 02:08:24.230
I think the 1946 experience was good for the boys because the minds were more stimulated

02:08:24.230 --> 02:08:28.290
there, there was no television or playstation games.

02:08:30.570 --> 02:08:34.790
I think Ben and Thomas could still live without the television and the PlayStation.

02:08:34.790 --> 02:08:43.530
On PlayStation games, you seem to get bored with them, but you can't rearrange them because they're on the computer.

02:08:43.530 --> 02:08:50.080
If you make your own games, you can rearrange it so you don't get bored.

02:08:50.080 --> 02:08:52.440
Right, should we close the hat so we've got more room?

02:08:52.440 --> 02:08:58.580
1940s house. They hardly ever argued and they did play together.

02:09:01.240 --> 02:09:03.600
We argued less in the 1940s.

02:09:04.560 --> 02:09:08.670
We don't know why, I just think it was probably because there was less to argue over.

02:09:09.380 --> 02:09:13.300
Because normal things we argue over is who gets to go on the PlayStation,

02:09:14.180 --> 02:09:17.580
who, silly stuff like Tom taking my things,

02:09:17.840 --> 02:09:21.040
because we didn't have any, Tom couldn't take anything.

02:09:21.400 --> 02:09:23.380
We didn't have a PlayStation to argue over.

02:09:23.380 --> 02:09:26.540
We shared them all, so there's nothing to take, really.

02:09:27.000 --> 02:09:28.900
It was half as yours and half as mine.

02:09:36.300 --> 02:09:42.090
Itmar has today joined the army and from a garrison theater somewhere in England, we say once more that man again

02:09:42.390 --> 02:09:49.500
With no television in their wartime home Ben and Thomas produced their own version of a famous wartime entertainment

02:09:49.500 --> 02:09:55.200
as it was a special occasion

02:09:55.960 --> 02:10:00.000
Thomas was allowed to borrow a modern torch to create his lighting effect

02:10:00.000 --> 02:10:10.410
And welcome to...

02:10:10.410 --> 02:10:11.830
It's the...

02:10:11.830 --> 02:10:12.750
Bean again!

02:10:22.940 --> 02:10:25.600
After you, Sizzle.

02:10:25.800 --> 02:10:27.420
I insist, Sizzle.

02:10:27.720 --> 02:10:29.140
No, I insist, Rob.

02:10:30.030 --> 02:10:36.870
Thomas, I feel, is the one that's gained the most from doing the 1940s.

02:10:37.280 --> 02:10:39.620
He's always felt that he was in the shadow of his brother

02:10:39.620 --> 02:10:42.080
and because of his speech problems as well.

02:10:43.030 --> 02:10:45.170
He's picked up a merit badge at school,

02:10:45.290 --> 02:10:48.650
which they only give out to one child out the whole of the year.

02:10:49.860 --> 02:10:52.300
I think he's the only child in the class that asks for homework.

02:10:53.380 --> 02:10:54.930
Anyway. Oh, yes.

02:10:57.300 --> 02:10:59.560
With the sizzle rub.

02:11:00.160 --> 02:11:07.210
Then when he comes in from school I don't have to constantly be at him to do his homework,

02:11:07.210 --> 02:11:10.830
he'll come in and do it straight away. And that has only happened since we came back

02:11:10.830 --> 02:11:12.830
from doing the 1940s experience.

02:11:12.830 --> 02:11:20.500
Yeah I wondered what a puppet looked like naked. There it is. It's a sad time of night.

02:11:20.500 --> 02:11:21.500
Aww.

02:11:21.500 --> 02:11:30.000
The end of the show. Aww.

02:11:30.000 --> 02:11:34.240
in general have become less demanding, I would think, in, you know, in everything, in sort

02:11:34.240 --> 02:11:38.820
of, you know, buying things and wanting things, they appreciate the things that they've got

02:11:38.820 --> 02:11:42.120
now, rather than constantly wanting more things all the time.

02:11:42.570 --> 02:11:43.070
Bye.

02:11:46.250 --> 02:11:51.910
Since they've been back, Michael and the boys have maintained their wartime habit of creating

02:11:51.910 --> 02:11:54.600
their own amusements out of nothing.

02:12:00.250 --> 02:12:13.260
Doodle Bug Alley is a board game that we invented after the 1940s, and it's basically just the Spitfires trying to protect London, and the Doodle Bugs trying to get through to get to London.

02:12:13.930 --> 02:12:18.750
We did a map of West Wycombe because they had to fly over West Wycombe to get to London.

02:12:20.460 --> 02:12:24.340
It's sort of like drafts, but with a different background.

02:12:24.340 --> 02:12:25.480
Oh, yeah, it's just in time.

02:12:30.860 --> 02:12:37.390
While I was in the 1940s house, I'd rediscovered the joy of writing,

02:12:37.390 --> 02:12:41.490
and as I wrote more and more letters and wrote a diary,

02:12:41.490 --> 02:12:44.830
I found that words and phrases were coming back to me.

02:12:44.830 --> 02:12:49.430
Whereas, prior to that, I couldn't string a sentence together some days,

02:12:49.430 --> 02:12:53.970
which I believed to be either advancing years or the menopause.

02:12:53.970 --> 02:12:57.720
But the reality was I wasn't using language to its full extent.

02:13:00.090 --> 02:13:06.450
I think letters are almost a form of a therapy because as you're writing, you're actually

02:13:06.450 --> 02:13:10.510
thinking about what you're saying, you're thinking about what you feel, whereas on a

02:13:10.510 --> 02:13:16.900
phone call it's easy to just glibly say, "Yeah, I'm fine, yeah, things great."

02:13:16.900 --> 02:13:20.950
There's more truth somehow in a letter than in a telephone call.

02:13:20.950 --> 02:13:26.090
Before the 1940s, my mum would go out, you could go have a drink with her, a few bottles

02:13:26.090 --> 02:13:30.000
of beer, she'd spend loads of money on clothes, food.

02:13:30.510 --> 02:13:39.890
anything. Now she's totally different.

02:13:39.890 --> 02:13:46.190
Lynne doesn't drive the car anymore and I'm very surprised she doesn't because it took

02:13:46.190 --> 02:13:53.390
her years and thousands upon thousands of pounds to pass and now she doesn't drive the

02:13:53.390 --> 02:14:00.000
car anywhere. She used to go to the supermarket an awful lot. In fact she was forever at the

02:14:00.250 --> 02:14:11.880
rotten place but isn't anymore she takes her basket that silly basket down to town every single day

02:14:11.880 --> 02:14:21.300
shopping at the greengrocers the bakery the butchers the biggest impact that the wartime

02:14:21.300 --> 02:14:30.000
experience has had on my current day living is that it's saving me so much money help you

02:14:31.240 --> 02:14:43.090
I save money on the petrol, I save money by buying fresh produce, this is a pre-packaged,

02:14:43.090 --> 02:14:54.190
I buy all my food fresh daily from local shops, not supermarkets.

02:14:54.190 --> 02:15:00.000
One of the things that was very important to me throughout the wartime experience was my

02:15:00.000 --> 02:15:06.720
Mr. Lovegrove. He was a port in a storm. If my circumstances were different and I

02:15:06.720 --> 02:15:14.170
was perhaps on my own or elderly then it would almost be a lifeline the local

02:15:14.170 --> 02:15:20.070
shopkeeper and I do feel very strongly that I must champion the cause of the

02:15:20.070 --> 02:15:25.300
local shopkeeper not only from a social point of view but because I found it to

02:15:25.300 --> 02:15:30.000
be cheaper. A comparison of Lynn's weekly shopping expenses before

02:15:30.000 --> 02:15:36.240
before and after her 1940s experience shows a dramatic change.

02:15:36.240 --> 02:15:41.580
Today, for the first time, I've actually sat down and compared

02:15:41.580 --> 02:15:47.340
my pre-war budget, my wartime budget, and my post-war budget,

02:15:47.340 --> 02:15:50.420
and I have to say I'm absolutely shocked.

02:15:50.420 --> 02:15:55.200
I've looked through my bank statements from before the war,

02:15:55.200 --> 02:15:58.020
which have all my shopping bills on.

02:15:58.020 --> 02:15:59.670
There's not one week

02:16:00.000 --> 02:16:03.810
where I spend less than £200 on food.

02:16:03.810 --> 02:16:07.840
Moving on, because I am still shocked,

02:16:07.840 --> 02:16:11.840
I've decided to look at my current bank statements

02:16:11.840 --> 02:16:19.280
and here it's much more hopeful for my future and for Michael's.

02:16:19.280 --> 02:16:24.030
I'm spending no more than £100 a week.

02:16:24.030 --> 02:16:29.410
Most weeks it's £70 or £80 on the food bill.

02:16:30.000 --> 02:16:35.090
So that, I've reduced the food bills by more than 50%.

02:16:35.090 --> 02:16:38.160
How do these vegetables, Kirsty, if you make the pudding?

02:16:40.260 --> 02:16:43.900
You didn't say you had to come make a pudding.

02:16:44.380 --> 02:16:47.780
No, but as you're here for Sunday lunch and you're so good at puddings, I thought today

02:16:47.780 --> 02:16:48.580
you could make the pudding.

02:16:49.070 --> 02:16:54.740
I miss the rumble that Mum used to make and the meals that Annie used to make.

02:16:55.540 --> 02:17:00.000
Well, Annie still makes the meals, 1940.

02:17:00.000 --> 02:17:04.000
but Mum doesn't normally make puddings anymore for us.

02:17:04.000 --> 02:17:08.620
Let me see what you've got in the fridge to make pudding with.

02:17:08.620 --> 02:17:11.650
I must say your fridge is looking empty.

02:17:11.650 --> 02:17:14.630
I defy you to find anything you can throw out.

02:17:14.630 --> 02:17:22.240
Every nook and cranny of this house is filled with possessions,

02:17:22.240 --> 02:17:25.240
things that we don't need, things that aren't useful,

02:17:25.240 --> 02:17:29.420
things that really scream...

02:17:30.280 --> 02:17:37.810
excess excess excess and to me that's what modern life now is it's excesses too many

02:17:37.810 --> 02:17:51.340
we're now on a sunday have a proper traditional sunday dinner with all the family there we were

02:17:51.340 --> 02:17:58.780
used to you know being over faced with food now we just you know get what what we're given and

02:17:58.780 --> 02:18:00.000
that's enough i think we're going to

02:18:00.000 --> 02:18:03.440
to accept that we just, you know, what's on our plate and that's it.

02:18:03.440 --> 02:18:19.360
The rest of the family signed up for the 1940s experience

02:18:19.360 --> 02:18:23.720
as they wanted to cure Michael of his rose-tinted view of the period.

02:18:23.720 --> 02:18:30.000
But things have not worked out quite as expected.

02:18:30.060 --> 02:18:35.380
When Dad was in the house, straight away, he was in the role of a 1940s man.

02:18:35.380 --> 02:18:36.330
Mm-hm.

02:18:36.330 --> 02:18:37.330
Straight away?

02:18:37.330 --> 02:18:38.500
Straight away.

02:18:38.500 --> 02:18:43.070
At the table, wanting his food on the table, and if the wife wasn't running around after

02:18:43.070 --> 02:18:45.060
him, then the daughter had to do.

02:18:45.060 --> 02:18:46.710
Oh, well.

02:18:46.710 --> 02:18:49.160
Would he knock me again, then?

02:18:49.160 --> 02:18:51.520
